package views

// Privacy is the public "/privacy" marketing page - Resonance's privacy
// policy. Same chrome as Pricing/HowItWorks (marketingHeader/
// marketingFooter, landing.css) but a plain single-column reading layout
// (landing-legal, landing.css) rather than the hero/feature-card marketing
// layout those pages use, since this is dense section-headed prose, not a
// pitch. See MarketingController.Privacy (routes it in) and
// middleware/auth.go's publicPrefixes (makes it reachable with no
// session, same as Pricing/HowItWorks).
templ Privacy() {
	<!DOCTYPE html>
	<html lang="en">
		@Head("Privacy Policy") {
			@marketingMeta("Privacy Policy", "How Resonance handles your church's Planning Center data: what's read live, what's cached, what's actually stored, and who it's shared with.", "/privacy")
			<link rel="stylesheet" href={ "/static/css/landing.css?v=" + AssetVersion }/>
		}
		<body class="landing">
			@marketingHeader()
			<main>
				<article class="landing-legal">
					<h1>Privacy Policy</h1>
					<p class="landing-legal-updated">Last updated September 11, 2026</p>
					<h2>Who runs this</h2>
					<p>
						Resonance is a one-person project, run by Derek Garnett. It's not
						a company - there's no team, no legal department, just one
						person building a tool for churches that use Planning Center
						Services. Questions, concerns, or requests about your data all go
						to the same place: support@derekgarnett.com.
					</p>
					<h2>Signing in</h2>
					<p>
						Resonance has no username-and-password system of its own. The
						only way to sign in is "Sign in with Planning Center" - an OAuth
						connection through Planning Center Online, the company behind
						Planning Center Services (Resonance isn't affiliated with them).
						Resonance never sees, asks for, or stores your Planning Center
						password.
					</p>
					<h2>What Resonance reads from Planning Center, and doesn't store</h2>
					<p>
						Once you're signed in, Resonance reads your church's own data
						live from Planning Center, as you, to show it back to you: people
						and team members, songs, plans, teams, positions, and service
						types. Planning Center stays the source of truth for all of it -
						Resonance isn't building its own copy of your library or
						schedule. A few pieces of it do get cached locally for a short
						time, purely to avoid re-fetching the same data on every page load
						and to stay under Planning Center's own API rate limit - see the
						next section.
					</p>
					<h2>What Resonance actually stores</h2>
					<p>
						Planning Center has no concept of a few things Resonance needs,
						so those live in Resonance's own database instead:
					</p>
					<ul>
						<li>
							Relationships and tags your church's admins define between
							songs or people - things like "these two songs flow well
							together" or "avoid scheduling these two people together" -
							plus roles/contexts for songs, scheduling preferences, and
							vocal range notes.
						</li>
						<li>
							Draft sets and rosters you're building, before they're
							published to real Planning Center plan items and assignments.
						</li>
					</ul>
					<p>
						A few of these write straight back to Planning Center the moment
						you set them - a position scheduling preference, for instance -
						because Planning Center has no draft concept for that field
						either. Most everything else stays purely local until you publish
						or refresh.
					</p>
					<h2>A short-lived performance cache</h2>
					<p>
						Some Planning Center data - song arrangement details like key,
						tempo, and length, and people's team-availability history - gets
						cached in Resonance's own database for a short time, purely so
						Resonance doesn't have to re-fetch it from Planning Center on
						every page load and so normal use doesn't run into Planning
						Center's rate limit on how many requests an account can make.
						This isn't new data collection; it's a temporary copy of data
						Planning Center already has, and it expires and refreshes
						automatically on its own.
					</p>
					<h2>Account and session data</h2>
					<p>Resonance also stores what it needs to keep your organization signed in and billed correctly:</p>
					<ul>
						<li>
							An organization record: your church's name, subscription
							status/plan, and the Stripe customer/subscription IDs used for
							billing. No payment card data itself - Stripe holds that.
						</li>
						<li>
							Session records that keep you signed in. Only a hashed,
							irreversible version of your session token is stored - never
							the raw token itself.
						</li>
						<li>
							The Planning Center OAuth access and refresh tokens needed to
							keep acting on your behalf while you're signed in.
						</li>
					</ul>
					<h2>Cookies</h2>
					<p>
						Resonance sets exactly one cookie, and it's first-party: the
						session cookie that keeps you signed in. There are no
						third-party advertising or tracking cookies, and no analytics or
						tracking scripts of any kind anywhere in the app.
					</p>
					<h2>Feedback you submit</h2>
					<p>
						If you use the feedback form, your message - and your email
						address, if you choose to give one - is stored in Resonance's own
						database and also emailed directly to Derek via Mailgun, a
						transactional email provider, so it can actually be read and
						responded to.
					</p>
					<h2>Who this gets shared with</h2>
					<p>Resonance shares data with exactly three outside services, each for a specific reason:</p>
					<ul>
						<li>
							<strong>Planning Center Online</strong> - your identity provider
							and the source of your church's own scheduling data. Resonance
							acts on it as you, authenticated through the OAuth connection
							you granted when you signed in.
						</li>
						<li>
							<strong>Stripe</strong> - handles payment processing and
							subscription billing. All actual payment details live with
							Stripe, not Resonance.
						</li>
						<li>
							<strong>Mailgun</strong> - sends the feedback emails described
							above.
						</li>
					</ul>
					<p>Resonance doesn't sell data to anyone, and there's no advertising of any kind.</p>
					<h2>Data retention and deletion</h2>
					<p>
						There's no self-serve "delete my data" button yet. If you want
						your organization's data deleted, or want to know exactly what's
						stored, email support@derekgarnett.com and it'll be handled by
						hand. Draft data - sets, rosters, relationships, preferences -
						stays until it's deleted this way or naturally superseded by a
						newer draft. Anything already published lives on in your church's
						own Planning Center account from that point on, governed by
						Planning Center's own retention, not Resonance's.
					</p>
					<h2>Children's privacy</h2>
					<p>Resonance isn't directed at children and isn't knowingly used by anyone under 13.</p>
					<h2>Changes to this policy</h2>
					<p>
						This policy may be updated from time to time. The "Last updated"
						date at the top is how to tell it changed. Anything material gets
						communicated in a reasonable way - noted in-app, for instance -
						not buried in a silent edit.
					</p>
					<h2>Questions</h2>
					<p>
						Derek runs Resonance out of Missouri, USA, and handles privacy
						questions and requests personally. Reach out any time at
						support@derekgarnett.com.
					</p>
				</article>
			</main>
			@marketingFooter()
		</body>
	</html>
}
